== Directions for Giving

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[I told you before that I am willing to do you a kindness: and that it may be a kindness indeed, I must farther tell you, not only what you should do, but #emph[how you must do it, that you may not loose your reward.] And if you would make sure to be gainers by whatever you thus lay out, observe the following #emph[DIRECTIONS.]]

#emph[I.] Give yourselves to the Lord, and with yourselves, all that ever you have, to be so laid out, whether upon yourselves or others, as he doth appoint and require.

2 Cor 8:2. The #emph[Apostle] boasting of the #emph[Macedonian Christians,] and of the riches of their liberality, tells us, v. 5. #emph[That they first gave themselves to the Lord.] He that will not give #emph[himself] to the Lord, is like to give but little else. And if he should give all that he had, and only withhold himself, God will not accept, nor reward it. God will have nothing of thee, if he may not have thy heart.

Give yourselves for #emph[servants] to the Lord, to serve him with your spirits first, and then with all that you have. Make over yourselves to God in Christ to be his #emph[covenant servants,] & thence-forth count and say of yourselves, as the #emph[Apostle] teacheth you, #emph[1 Cor 6:20. I am not mine own, but must henceforth glorify God with my body and my spirit, yea and with all that I have, which are his.]

He knows not what he says, that says #emph[I serve the Lord, who hath not first given himself to him for a servant;] God will not be served by aliens. And he hath but lied unto God, who says, #emph[I give myself unto the Lord for a servant,] and doth not actually serve him with all that he hath. He that hath given #emph[himself] to the Lord, will withhold nothing from him that God will have.

He that will serve the Lord with his spirit, will as readily serve him with all that he hath. God that hath given us his #emph[Son,] how #emph[will he not with him freely give us all things,] Rom 1:32? And that man that hath given God his soul, will keep back nothing from him.

Friend, thou sayst thou art the #emph[Lord's,] and hast devoted thyself to him; thou hopest that thou art Christ's, and hast joined thyself in covenant unto him, resolving to be his disciple: but, which way runs thine estate? Whose house is this, these lands, and this money which thou hast, are these the Lord's too? How is it then that thy #emph[lusts] have so much of them at their service? That thy #emph[pride] hath so much, and thine appetite so much, or that they are all impropriated and kept by thee to feed thy #emph[covetousness?] Is all thou hast the #emph[Lord's?] How is it then that thou begrutchest, or thinkest-so-much of every little that he calls for from thee? Thou who wilt not in every case part with so much as God calls for, art either a liar, or a robber. Either thou didst not give all to God, when thou saydst thou didst, and then thou art a #emph[liar:] or if thou didst give all, and yet will not let him have what he calls for, but bestowest it elsewhere, then thou art a #emph[robber.]

#emph[Obi.] But some will say, must I give all I have to the poor, and so leave myself, and my farily to be beggars? Or, what else is your meaning, when you say, I must give all to the Lord?

My meaning is, #emph[that you so give all to the Lord, as to resolve to dispose of your whole estate to such persons and purposes, as God orders and appoints you.] The Lord would have you live, and maintain your self, and provide for your family soberly, according to your rank and degree (some extraordinary cases being excepted.) but still you must allow no more to your self, nor no less to those in need, then is according to the will, and good-pleasure of the Lord.

Some other will here put in and say: but I am free and bountiful, and give great alas; I cast my bread upon the waters; I give a portion to six, and also to seven: I devise liberal things, I delight to show mercy.

Dost thou so? 'Tis well thou dost, God's blessing on thine heart for it: 'tis great pity that any liberal man in the world should lose his reward; and that thou maist not lose thine, take this counsel of a friend that loves thee, see that thou hast sincerely given up thyself to God as his #emph[covenant-servant,] and art a devoted disciple of Jesus Christ, and that these thy works of mercy are done in pursuance of thy covenant, as a part of that service which thou owest and hast vowed to thy Lord, whose thou art, and whose talents thou reckonest all thou hast. And look to this the rather, because its possible that men of great good works may be, as of little charity, so of little faith also, which is that grace which entitles God to us, and all we do, and obtains our acceptance with him. First, by faith give thyself to him, and then by charity serve him with what thou hast, and then doubt not of a plentiful return.

II. #emph[Offer up your gift upon the altar.] Give yourselves, and with yourselves, all that you have, #emph[through Christ,] unto God. Let him be #emph[your altar that sanctifies your gift:] 'tis through him alone you will be accepted, #emph[Eph 1:6. He hath made us accepted in the beloved.] Let your #emph[Souls be sprinkled with his blood,] and thereby washed from your blood, from your natural and contracted guilt, and pollution, and then they will be a gift acceptable to the Lord. Do not think you have no more to do, but to give yourselves as you are to the Lord: you have given yourselves to sin, and now, it may be you think, you will give yourselves back to the Lord: but know there is more in it than so. Thou art in thyself a guilty, and defiled soul, and God will have none of thee in this case: go to #emph[Christ] first, and get him to cover thine unrighteousness, and to cleanse thee from thy filthiness, put thy wretched soul into his hands, and let him present it unto the Father.

And what ever thou givest with thyself, #emph[thine alms, thy bread,] or #emph[thy flesh,] or #emph[thy money,] that thou hast for his poor; put it all into the same hand, and let him present this also to the Father for the use of his servants.

Friends, though I would have you givers yet I would not have you losers. I would not have you losers, either by #emph[saving,] or by #emph[unprofitable giving.]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. #emph[Be not losers by saving.] That's the next way to #emph[lose all,] by thinking to #emph[save, and keep all to yourselves. He that will save his life,] that is, by not laying it down when God calls for it, #emph[he shall loose it.] And so #emph[he that will save his estate,] that is, #emph[by refusing to lay it out where] God would have him, #emph[he is the more like to loose all that he hath.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. #emph[Lose not by unprofitable bestowing.] All that is bestowed unprofitably, as to thee, to whomsoever thou givest it, which is not given first through Christ unto God, and to his servants for the Lord's sake. It may be profit however to them, who receive it, but no profit to thee who givest. 'Tis only whats given to God, and to men for God's sake, for which God becomes debtor.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[III. #emph[Fetch all your alms out of your hearts. Draw forth thy soul to the hungry,] Isa 58:10. Give what thou givest, 1, #emph[Out of a willing heart.] 2, #emph[Out of a compassionate heart. 3. Out of a thankfful heart:]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. #emph[Out of a willing heart; give willingly,] 2 Cor 9:6. #emph[Every man as he purposeth in his heart so let him give, not grudgingly, or of necessity, for God loueth a cheerful giver.] An alms-deed in Scripture is called a #emph[sacrifice,] such a sacrifice as is well-pleasing unto God, Heb 13:16: #emph[to do good, and to communicate forget not, for with such sacrifices God is well pleased.] Now all our Christian sacrifices are to be #emph[free-will oblations.] An alms without a will, is a sacrifice without an heart, and will be rejected of God.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. #emph[Out of a compassionate heart.] Get a compassionate heart, and fetch all thine alms out of thine own bowels. #emph['Tis the hard-hearts of men] that shut up their #emph[purses:] thou say'st, thou hast not to spare, thou wantest it thyself, or those about thee may want it: #emph[no, thou wantest an heart,] and that is the reason that those that need, must want #emph[thine alms.] A compassionate heart would find something, or other, for those that are in distress.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Get a compassionate heart. God is a God of compassion, and his children are compassionate children, #emph[Col 3:12. Put on, (as the elect of God) bowels of mercy. 1 John 3:17. Whoso hath this world's goods, and seeth his brother in need, and shuteth up his bowels of compassion from him, (or is a man of no bowels,) how dwelleth the love of God in him?] What ever thy profession, or thy religion be, or thy hopes be concerning it, thou hast nothing of God in thee, thou art none of his, who hast no bowels. God is a God of mercy, his children are all merciful children.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Worldling, wilt thou prove thyself to be #emph[no child of God?] Wouldst thou prove that the #emph[faith] thou hast is #emph[vain,] thy #emph[religion vain,] and that thou art but #emph[a pretender to the love of God,] and hast not the love of God within thee? This unmercifulness of thine will put it out of question. If thou hast no bowels of compassion, the love of God is not in thee.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[O get a compassionate, and merciful heart. Hast thou received mercy, and wilt thou not be merciful? Dost thou hope for mercy, and wilt thou not show mercy? Put thyself into thy poor brother's case a while, think how hard tis with him, think of #emph[his hunger] and #emph[nakedness,] of #emph[his pressing straits and miseries,] let thy soul go into his house, and see #emph[his naked walls, his cold chimney, his empty cupboard, his starveling children:] and then think again O how if it were thus with me! Ah poor distressed creatures: how are they #emph[pinched] and #emph[pined] whilst I have enough and abound? O my #emph[bowels] how can you but yern? O mine #emph[heart,] how canst thou but bleed over such distressed ones? Hast thou nothing to help them? There's enough in my house there's enough in my purse to yield them relief, but is there no alms for them in my heart? Can I have the heart to see them pine and perish, and do nothing to help them? Where are ye, O my bowels? Where are my compassions? O my soul help, and send portions to them for whom nothing is provided.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. #emph[Out of a thankful heart.] Remember what God hath done for thee; hast thou any sense or experience of his special love to thee, in Christ? What should that produce? The sense of divine goodness in a way of #emph[Common providence] calls for all due acknowledgment from us: but a #emph[taste] of his #emph[paternal love in pardoning our sins, and justifying our persons, and enstating us in eternal life and happiness, upon the account of his Son's-suffering, and dying for us,] this is, as the strongest #emph[Motive] to, so the most #emph[commanding reason] of our charity, or beneficence to our fellow-creatures, who stand in need of it.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[In Christ, the beams of God's mercy are concentred, as the beams of the sun are in a burning-glass, which falling upon our hearts, they are, or should be hereby inflamed, and made to burn with greater heat of love and kindness to all in want, especially to all who bear his image. #emph[The right spring of mercy to our neighbor, is the sense of God's mercy towards us.]]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[IV. #emph[Let all your streams of love flow into the ocean.] My meaning is, #emph[let all be done to the glory of God.] This must be your last end to which all must be directed, as the Apostle charges, #emph[1 Cor 10:31. Whether you eat or drink,] I may add, or whether you give to eat, or to drink, #emph[or whatsoever ye do, do all to the glory of God.] Let this be chiefly in your eye, and your heart, #emph[that God may be glorified.] Other subordinate ends there are, as #emph[the refreshing of the needy,] the #emph[adorning our holy profession,] the #emph[edification of others by our good example,] but all at last must end here, #emph[that God may be glorified.]]

Take heed your end be not to glorify yourselves. As #emph[worms] breed in the fairest fruit; so #emph[pride,] and #emph[vain-glory] are apt to creep-up out of the best duties.

V. #emph[Let your works of charity be done in humility, giving unto God the honor of them, by acknowledging that as what you have bestowed on the poor, you first received from him: so it was his goodness to give you an heart to give anything out of your abundance towards their relief.] Yea, you ought to be ever jealous over yourselves, lest there hath been some #emph[hypocrisy,] and #emph[self-seeking] in your works of #emph[charity;] acknowledging that you are so far from meriting #emph[heaven,] and #emph[salvation by your good works,] that, if God should deal with you according to the rigor of his justice, he might cast you into #emph[hell,] for that #emph[pride] and #emph[hypocrisy] which cleaves to your best works.

VI. #emph[Let your works of charity to men be accompanied with prayers, and thanksgivings unto God.] Thank God, that he hath put you amongst the #emph[givers,] and not amongst the #emph[receivers,] it being #emph[a more blessed thing to give then to receive:] that he hath put you among the #emph[givers,] and not the #emph[withholders:] that he hath given you an #emph[estate] to give, and an #emph[heart] to give. Thank God, that he will accept a gift at your hands; that he, whose is all you have, and to whom it is owing, will count that a gift, which is but the payment of so small a part of your debt: say with #emph[David,] in #emph[1 Chronicles 29:11, 13. Thine, O Lord, is the greatness: both riches and honor come from thee; now therefore, our God, we thank thee, and praise thy glorious name. But what am I, and what is my people, that we should be able to offer so willingly after this sort? For all things come of thee, and of thine own have we given thee.]

VII. #emph[Give out proportionably to what God hath given unto you,] 1 Cor 16:2. The Apostle adviseth the #emph[Corinthians] to give, #emph[as God had prospered them.]

Rich men therefore ought to be #emph[rich in good works,] for God expecteth fruit answerable to the seed which he soweth. Hath he #emph[abounded] to you in this world's goods? You ought thereupon to be #emph[abundant] in good works towards others: your pounds are #emph[expected,] where the widows mites are #emph[accepted.]
